The BUDT Solver Engine: A Complete Theoretical Framework for Bounded-Uncertainty Decision Theory

ouadi Maakoul 

AI :  Deepseek, Gemini 

Abstract

We present a complete theoretical treatment of the Bounded-Uncertainty Decision Theory (BUDT) solver engine, a constrained partially observable Markov decision process framework in which belief compression, resource-aware value estimation, calibrated action selection, and constrained policy optimisation are placed on equal footing. We establish rigorous contraction properties of the bounded-resource Bellman operator under both strict and non-strict depletion regimes, prove existence and measurable selection of optimal policies, derive distribution-free coverage guarantees via adaptive conformal inference, establish Bayesian and frequentist regret bounds with minimax-optimal rates, and provide a two-time-scale stochastic approximation analysis for the joint actor–critic–dual–encoder system. The framework integrates learned belief compression, resource-aware value estimation, constrained policy optimisation, and conformal calibration into a single coherent Lagrangian architecture with provable safety and near-optimality guarantees.

---

1. Problem Formulation

1.1 The Augmented State Space

Let \mathcal{S} be a Polish state space, \mathcal{O} a Polish observation space, and \mathcal{A} a compact metric action space. The agent operates in a resource-constrained partially observable environment. The augmented state is

X_t=(m_t,\rho_t)\in\mathcal{M}\times\mathcal{R},

where m_t\in\mathcal{M} is the memory state (a finite-dimensional sufficient statistic of the observation history) and \rho_t\in\mathcal{R}\subset\mathbb{R}^K_+ is the resource vector. We assume:

· \mathcal{M} is a compact metric space.
· \mathcal{R}=[0,\rho_{\max}]^K is compact and convex.
· \mathcal{A} is a compact metric space.

The environment is characterised by a transition kernel P(ds'\mid s,a), an observation kernel O(do\mid s,a), and a reward function r:\mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{A}\to\mathbb{R}. The resource cost is \mathcal{C}:\mathcal{A}\times\mathcal{M}\to\mathbb{R}^K_+.

1.2 The Constrained Objective

The agent selects actions a_t\in\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{valid}}(m_t,\rho_t), where

\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{valid}}(m,\rho)=\{a\in\mathcal{A}:\mathcal{C}(a,m)\le\rho\}.

The resource-adjusted return is

J(\pi)=\mathbb{E}_\pi\left[\sum_{t=0}^\infty\gamma^t\big(r(s_t,a_t)-\lambda^\top\mathcal{C}(a_t,m_t)\big)\right],

where \gamma\in(0,1) and \lambda\in\mathbb{R}^K_+ is the vector of Lagrange multipliers. The resource constraint is

\mathbb{E}_\pi\left[\sum_{t=0}^\infty\gamma^t\mathcal{C}(a_t,m_t)\right]\le\rho_0.

1.3 The Bounded-Resource Bellman Equation

The optimal value function V^\star:\mathcal{M}\times\mathcal{R}\to\mathbb{R} satisfies

V^\star(m,\rho)=\max_{a\in\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{valid}}(m,\rho)}\mathbb{E}\left[r(s,a)-\lambda^\top\mathcal{C}(a,m)+\gamma V^\star(m',\rho')\mid m,\rho,a\right].

We approximate V^\star by V_\theta:\mathcal{M}\times\mathcal{R}\to\mathbb{R} with parameters \theta\in\Theta\subseteq\mathbb{R}^p, and the policy by \pi_\phi:\mathcal{M}\times\mathcal{R}\to\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{A}) with parameters \phi\in\Phi\subseteq\mathbb{R}^q.

