2. Mathematical Preliminaries and Assumptions

2.1 Assumptions

Assumption 1 (Spaces). \mathcal{S},\mathcal{O} are Polish, \mathcal{A} is compact metric, \mathcal{M} is compact metric, \mathcal{R}=[0,\rho_{\max}]^K is compact convex.

Assumption 2 (Continuity). The kernels P(ds'\mid s,a), O(do\mid s,a), the reward r(s,a), and the cost \mathcal{C}(a,m) are continuous in all arguments. The belief update \mathcal{B}(b,a,o') is continuous in the weak topology on \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{S}).

Assumption 3 (Strict Depletion). There exist constants c_{\min}>\varepsilon_{\max}\ge 0 such that for all a,m, \|\mathcal{C}(a,m)\|_1\ge c_{\min} and \|\epsilon_t\|_1\le\varepsilon_{\max}.

Assumption 4 (Interior Feasibility). For every (m,\rho)\in\mathcal{M}\times\mathcal{R}, there exists a\in\mathcal{A} and a uniform \delta>0 such that \mathcal{C}(a,m)\le\rho-\delta\mathbf{1} componentwise.

Assumption 5 (Approximation Error). There exists \varepsilon_{\mathrm{approx}}<\infty such that

\sup_{V\in\mathcal{V}}\|\Pi_\Theta V-V\|_{L^2(\mu)}\le\varepsilon_{\mathrm{approx}}.

Assumption 6 (Boundedness). r, \mathcal{C}, and V_\theta are uniformly bounded on their domains.

Assumption 7 (Lipschitz Continuity). r,\mathcal{C},V_\theta,\pi_\phi,\phi_{\mathrm{enc}},\phi_{\mathrm{comp}} are Lipschitz continuous with constants L_r,L_c,L_V,L_\pi,L_{\mathrm{enc}},L_{\mathrm{comp}}.

Assumption 8 (Learning Rates). The learning rates satisfy the Robbins–Monro conditions

\sum_t\alpha_t=\infty,\quad\sum_t\alpha_t^2<\infty,\quad\sum_t\beta_t=\infty,\quad\sum_t\beta_t^2<\infty.

Assumption 9 (Ergodicity). For every stationary policy \pi, the Markov chain \{X_t\} is ergodic with stationary distribution \mu_\pi.

2.2 Continuity of the Belief Update

The belief update is

\mathcal{B}(b,a,o')(ds)=\frac{O(do'\mid s,a)\,b(ds)}{\int_{\mathcal{S}}O(do'\mid s,a)\,b(ds')}.

Under Assumption 2, the denominator is continuous and bounded away from zero on the support of O, so \mathcal{B} is continuous in the weak topology. The compressed memory m=\psi(b,\rho) inherits continuity from \psi with modulus L_{\mathrm{comp}}, yielding

\|\mathcal{B}(b,a,o')-\mathcal{B}(b',a,o')\|_{\mathrm{TV}}\le\frac{2\|O\|_\infty}{\eta_{\min}}\|b-b'\|_{\mathrm{TV}},

where \eta_{\min}>0 is a lower bound on the observation likelihood.

2.3 Continuity of the Feasible Correspondence

Lemma 1 (Berge). Under Assumptions 2 and 4, the correspondence (m,\rho)\mapsto\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{valid}}(m,\rho) is continuous (both upper and lower hemicontinuous).

Proof. Upper hemicontinuity follows from closedness of \{(a,m,\rho):\mathcal{C}(a,m)\le\rho\}. For lower hemicontinuity, let (m_n,\rho_n)\to(m,\rho) and a\in\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{valid}}(m,\rho). By Assumption 4, there exists a^\delta with \mathcal{C}(a^\delta,m)\le\rho-\delta\mathbf{1}. Define a_n=(1-t_n)a+t_n a^\delta with t_n\to0 chosen so that \mathcal{C}(a_n,m_n)\le\rho_n. Continuity of \mathcal{C} and convexity of \mathcal{A} yield a_n\to a, establishing lower hemicontinuity. \square

