3. The Bounded-Resource Bellman Operator

3.1 Definition

Define the operator \mathcal{T}^{\mathcal{B}}:\mathcal{V}\to\mathcal{V} by

(\mathcal{T}^{\mathcal{B}}V)(m,\rho)=\max_{a\in\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{valid}}(m,\rho)}\mathbb{E}\left[r(s,a)-\lambda^\top\mathcal{C}(a,m)+\gamma V(m',\rho')\mid m,\rho,a\right],

where the expectation is with respect to the joint law of (s,o',\varepsilon):

\mathbb{E}[\cdot\mid m,\rho,a]=\int_{\mathcal{S}}\int_{\mathcal{O}}\int_{\mathcal{R}}(\cdot)\,P(ds\mid m)\,O(do'\mid s,a)\,\nu(d\varepsilon).

3.2 Contraction Under Strict Depletion

Theorem 1 (Contraction). Under Assumptions 2 and 3, \mathcal{T}^{\mathcal{B}} is a \gamma-contraction in \|\cdot\|_\infty:

\|\mathcal{T}^{\mathcal{B}}V_1-\mathcal{T}^{\mathcal{B}}V_2\|_\infty\le\gamma\|V_1-V_2\|_\infty.

Proof. For fixed (m,\rho,a), the reward and cost terms cancel in the difference:

\left|\mathbb{E}[r-\lambda^\top\mathcal{C}+\gamma V_1]-\mathbb{E}[r-\lambda^\top\mathcal{C}+\gamma V_2]\right|\le\gamma\|V_1-V_2\|_\infty.

Taking the supremum over a\in\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{valid}}(m,\rho) and then over (m,\rho) preserves the bound. \square

Corollary 1 (Existence). Under Assumptions 2–4, there exists a unique V^\star\in\mathcal{V} with \mathcal{T}^{\mathcal{B}}V^\star=V^\star.

Theorem 2 (Optimal Policy). The greedy policy

\pi^\star(m,\rho)=\arg\max_{a\in\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{valid}}(m,\rho)}\mathbb{E}\left[r(s,a)-\lambda^\top\mathcal{C}(a,m)+\gamma V^\star(m',\rho')\right]

is measurable and satisfies V^{\pi^\star}=V^\star.

Proof. By Lemma 1, \mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{valid}} is a continuous correspondence with compact values. The Measurable Maximum Theorem (Aliprantis–Border, Theorem 18.19) yields a measurable selector. The equality V^{\pi^\star}=V^\star follows from the standard Bellman optimality argument. \square

3.3 Perov Extension for Non-Strict Depletion

When Assumption 3 is dropped, trajectories may wander to arbitrarily large resource levels. We introduce the weighted supremum norm

\|V\|_\kappa=\sup_{m,\rho}\frac{|V(m,\rho)|}{\kappa(\rho)},\qquad\kappa(\rho)=\exp\left(\sum_{i=1}^K\kappa_i\rho_i\right),

with \kappa_i>0 chosen so that

\gamma\sup_{m,\rho,a}\frac{\mathbb{E}[\kappa(\rho')\mid m,\rho,a]}{\kappa(\rho)}<1.

Theorem 3 (Perov Contraction). Under Assumptions 2, 5, 6, and the weight condition above, \mathcal{T}^{\mathcal{B}} is a contraction in the Perov sense: there exists a matrix M\in\mathbb{R}^{K\times K}_+ with spectral radius \rho(M)<1 such that

\|\mathcal{T}^{\mathcal{B}}V_1-\mathcal{T}^{\mathcal{B}}V_2\|_\kappa\le M\|V_1-V_2\|_\kappa.

Consequently, \mathcal{T}^{\mathcal{B}} admits a unique fixed point in the weighted space.

Proof. The expectation \mathbb{E}[\kappa(\rho')\mid m,\rho,a] is controlled by the resource dynamics \rho'=\Pi_{\mathcal{R}}(\rho-\mathcal{C}(a,m)+\epsilon). Under the weight condition, the operator is a vector-valued contraction, and the Perov fixed-point theorem applies. \square

3.4 Transient Bound Under Strict Depletion

Under Assumption 3, the expected time to reach a \delta-neighbourhood of the boundary is

\mathbb{E}[\tau_\delta]\le\frac{\|\rho_0\|_1}{c_{\min}-\varepsilon_{\max}}+O(1).

This bound is used in the finite-horizon approximation error analysis.

