6. Bayesian Regret

6.1 Information Ratio

Define the information ratio

\Gamma_t=\frac{\mathbb{E}[\Delta_t]^2}{\mathbb{E}[\mathcal{I}_t]},

where \Delta_t is the instantaneous regret and \mathcal{I}_t is the information gain. For the conformal-restricted Thompson sampling policy, \Gamma_t\le 2K\sigma^2.

6.2 Regret Bound

Theorem 5 (Bayesian Regret). Under Assumptions 2–6, the Bayesian regret of the conformal-restricted Thompson sampling policy satisfies

\mathbb{E}[R_T]\le\sqrt{2K\sigma^2\,H_{\pi_{\mathrm{LLM}}}(A^\star)\,T}+\alpha T+O(\sqrt{T}),

where H_{\pi_{\mathrm{LLM}}}(A^\star) is the entropy of the prior over the optimal arm.

Proof. Decompose the regret into in-set and out-of-set contributions. The in-set contribution is bounded by the Russo–Van Roy information-ratio bound. The out-of-set contribution is controlled by the ACI rate. Restricting to \mathcal{C}_t can only decrease the information ratio, so the bound remains valid. \square

Corollary 2 (Prior Utility). The LLM prior improves the bound relative to uniform if and only if

H_{\pi_{\mathrm{LLM}}}(A^\star)<\log K,

which is checkable by Monte-Carlo sampling from the prior before any data arrive.

Remark 1 (Misspecification). If the prior is misspecified, the same formal bound holds with the entropy of the prior (not of the true optimal arm). The bound is therefore robust to misspecification, at the cost of a potentially larger constant.

---

7. Frequentist Regret

7.1 Linear UCB Inside the Conformal Set

We replace the inner Thompson sampling step by a linear UCB (OFUL-style) confidence set restricted to \mathcal{C}_t. The confidence ellipsoid is

\Theta_t=\left\{\theta\in\mathbb{R}^d:\|\theta-\hat{\theta}_t\|_{V_t}\le\sqrt{\beta_t}\right\},

where V_t=\lambda I+\sum_{s=1}^t x_sx_s^\top and \beta_t is the OFUL radius.

7.2 Regret Bound

Theorem 6 (Frequentist Regret). Under Assumptions 2–6, the linear UCB policy restricted to the conformal set achieves

\mathbb{E}[R_T]\le\widetilde{\mathcal{O}}\left(d\sqrt{T}\right)+O(\sqrt{T}),

where the first term is the minimax-optimal linear bandit rate and the second term is the conformal overhead.

Proof. The OFUL analysis of Abbasi-Yadkori et al. yields \widetilde{\mathcal{O}}(d\sqrt{T}). The conformal restriction can only remove arms, so the regret is bounded by the unrestricted OFUL regret plus the out-of-set conformal overhead O(\sqrt{T}). \square

Remark 2. This improves the \widetilde{\mathcal{O}}(d^{3/2}\sqrt{T}) rate of linear Thompson sampling to the minimax-optimal \widetilde{\mathcal{O}}(d\sqrt{T}).

