8. Two-Time-Scale Actor–Critic–Dual–Encoder Convergence

8.1 Two-Time-Scale Separation

We adopt a two-time-scale stochastic approximation framework:

· Fast scale: critic parameters \theta and dual variables \lambda.
· Slow scale: actor parameters \phi and encoder parameters \theta_{\mathrm{enc}}.

Let \alpha_t be the fast learning rate and \beta_t the slow learning rate, with

\frac{\beta_t}{\alpha_t}\to0,\qquad\sum_t\alpha_t=\infty,\qquad\sum_t\alpha_t^2<\infty,\qquad\sum_t\beta_t=\infty,\qquad\sum_t\beta_t^2<\infty.

8.2 Fast-Scale Convergence (Critic and Dual)

For fixed (\phi,\theta_{\mathrm{enc}}), the critic update is

\theta_{t+1}=\theta_t+\alpha_t\delta_t\nabla_\theta V_\theta(m_t,\rho_t),

and the dual update is

\lambda_{t+1}=\left[\lambda_t+\eta_t\left(\mathbb{E}_\pi\left[\sum_{s=t}^\infty\gamma^{s-t}\mathcal{C}(a_s,m_s)\right]-\rho_0\right)\right]_+.

Theorem 7 (Critic Convergence). Under Assumptions 5–9, the TD(0) update with function approximation converges to a neighbourhood of the fixed point:

\lim_{t\to\infty}\mathbb{E}\left[\|V_{\theta_t}-V^\star\|_{L^2(\mu)}\right]\le\frac{\varepsilon_{\mathrm{approx}}}{1-\gamma}.

Theorem 8 (Dual Convergence). Under Assumptions 5–9, the dual update converges to the optimal Lagrange multiplier:

\lim_{t\to\infty}\lambda_t=\lambda^\star,

where \lambda^\star solves the dual problem

\lambda^\star=\arg\min_{\lambda\ge0}\max_{\phi\in\Phi}\mathcal{L}(\phi,\lambda).

Proof. The Lagrangian is convex in \lambda by the zero-duality-gap property of constrained MDPs under the Slater condition (Assumption 4). The projected stochastic gradient descent on the dual function converges to \lambda^\star under the Robbins–Monro conditions. The sign of the update ensures that \lambda increases when the constraint is violated. \square

8.3 Slow-Scale Convergence (Actor and Encoder)

For fixed (\theta,\lambda), the actor update is a stochastic approximation of the gradient flow

\dot{\phi}=\nabla_\phi\mathcal{L}(\phi,\lambda),

and the encoder update is

\theta_{\mathrm{enc}}\leftarrow\theta_{\mathrm{enc}}-\zeta\nabla_{\theta_{\mathrm{enc}}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{enc}}.

Theorem 9 (Actor Convergence). Under Assumptions 5–9, the policy gradient update with trust-region projection converges to a stationary point of the Lagrangian:

\lim_{t\to\infty}\|\nabla_\phi\mathcal{L}(\phi_t,\lambda_t)\|=0.

Theorem 10 (Encoder Convergence). Under Assumptions 5–9, the encoder primal–dual dynamics converge to a saddle point of the information-constrained Lagrangian.

8.4 Joint Convergence

Theorem 11 (Joint Convergence). Under Assumptions 5–9 and the two-time-scale separation, the joint iterates (\theta_t,\lambda_t,\phi_t,\theta_{\mathrm{enc},t}) converge to a stationary point of the coupled system:

\lim_{t\to\infty}\mathbb{E}\left[\|\theta_t-\theta^\star\|+\|\lambda_t-\lambda^\star\|+\|\phi_t-\phi^\star\|+\|\theta_{\mathrm{enc},t}-\theta_{\mathrm{enc}}^\star\|\right]=0.

Proof. By the two-time-scale stochastic approximation theorem (Borkar, 1997; Kushner–Yin, 2003), the fast-scale iterates converge to their fixed point conditional on the slow-scale iterates, and the slow-scale iterates converge to a stationary point of the averaged ODE. The ergodicity Assumption 9 ensures that the empirical measure converges to the stationary measure of the limiting policy. \square

8.5 Stochastic Dual Gradient

In practice, the expected cumulative cost is not observed. We replace it with a stochastic estimate \hat{c}_t (e.g., an eligibility trace or Monte-Carlo roll-out). The dual update becomes

\lambda_{t+1}=\left[\lambda_t+\eta_t(\hat{c}_t-\rho_0)\right]_+.

If \hat{c}_t is unbiased, the safety guarantee of Theorem 12 holds with an enlarged constant. If \hat{c}_t is asymptotically unbiased, the bias enters the O(1/\sqrt{T}) term.

