10. Complexity Analysis

10.1 Per-Iteration Complexity

The per-iteration complexity is dominated by:

1. Forward pass of V_\theta: O(p).
2. Forward pass of \pi_\phi: O(q).
3. Belief encoder: O(d_{\mathrm{enc}}).
4. Conformal quantile computation: O(n\log n) for sorting the calibration window.
5. Action selection: O(K) for the argmax over the feasible set.

Total per-iteration complexity:

O(p+q+d_{\mathrm{enc}}+n\log n+K).

10.2 Memory Complexity

O(p+q+d_{\mathrm{enc}}+n)

for storing the network parameters and the calibration window.

10.3 Sample Complexity

Under the assumptions of the convergence analysis, the number of iterations required to achieve \varepsilon-accuracy in the value function is

T_\varepsilon=O\left(\frac{1}{(1-\gamma)^2\varepsilon^2}\log\frac{1}{\delta}\right),

where \delta is the confidence parameter. This matches the standard sample complexity of approximate value iteration with function approximation.

