12. Concluding Remarks

The BUDT solver engine provides a complete algorithmic realisation of Bounded-Uncertainty Decision Theory, with rigorous convergence guarantees, safety constraints, and near-optimality bounds. The framework integrates learned belief compression, resource-aware value estimation, constrained policy optimisation, and conformal calibration into a single coherent Lagrangian architecture. The key theoretical contributions are:

1. Rigorous continuity via the interior-feasibility condition (Assumption 4), which guarantees lower hemicontinuity of the feasible correspondence and enables the application of Berge's Maximum Theorem.
2. Robust contraction via the Perov fixed-point theorem for the non-strict-depletion regime, extending the framework beyond the strict-depletion case.
3. Optimal frequentist regret via linear UCB inside the conformal set, achieving the minimax rate \widetilde{\mathcal{O}}(d\sqrt{T}).
4. Joint convergence via two-time-scale stochastic approximation, handling the non-stationarity induced by simultaneous updates of critic, actor, dual, and encoder.
5. Safety and near-optimality with explicit O(1/\sqrt{T}) rates, derived from the dual convergence and the performance difference lemma.

The framework is robust to misspecification of the LLM prior, handles the conformal fallback without breaking coverage, and treats the encoder as a constrained optimisation problem within the same Lagrangian architecture. The conceptual contribution—placing resource constraints, belief compression, and calibrated selection on equal footing with classical POMDP control—is thus given a rigorous mathematical foundation with provable guarantees.

Appendices to the BUDT Solver Engine

Appendix A: Continuity of the Belief Update and Feasible Correspondence

A.1 Belief Update Continuity

Let \mathcal{B}:\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{S})\times\mathcal{A}\times\mathcal{O}\to\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{S}) denote the Bayes belief update:

\mathcal{B}(b,a,o')(ds)=\frac{O(do'\mid s,a)\,b(ds)}{\int_{\mathcal{S}}O(do'\mid s,a)\,b(ds')}.

Lemma A.1 (Continuity of the Bayes Map). Under Assumption 2, the map (b,a,o')\mapsto\mathcal{B}(b,a,o') is continuous in the weak topology on \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{S}).

Proof. Let (b_n,a_n,o_n')\to(b,a,o') weakly. For any bounded continuous f:\mathcal{S}\to\mathbb{R},

\int f(s)\,\mathcal{B}(b_n,a_n,o_n')(ds)=\frac{\int f(s)O(do_n'\mid s,a_n)\,b_n(ds)}{\int O(do_n'\mid s,a_n)\,b_n(ds')}.

By Assumption 2, O(do'\mid s,a) is continuous in s and a, so s\mapsto O(do_n'\mid s,a_n) converges uniformly to s\mapsto O(do'\mid s,a). Since b_n\to b weakly and the integrands are bounded continuous, the numerator and denominator converge to their respective limits. The denominator is bounded below by \eta_{\min}>0 (the minimum observation likelihood on the support), so the quotient converges. \square

Corollary A.1 (Total Variation Bound). For any b,b'\in\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{S}),

\|\mathcal{B}(b,a,o')-\mathcal{B}(b',a,o')\|_{\mathrm{TV}}\le\frac{2\|O\|_\infty}{\eta_{\min}}\|b-b'\|_{\mathrm{TV}}.

A.2 Feasible Correspondence Continuity

Lemma A.2 (Berge's Maximum Theorem Application). Under Assumptions 2 and 4, the correspondence \Gamma(m,\rho)=\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{valid}}(m,\rho) is continuous (both upper and lower hemicontinuous).

Proof. Upper hemicontinuity. The graph of \Gamma is

\mathrm{Gr}(\Gamma)=\{(m,\rho,a):\mathcal{C}(a,m)\le\rho\}.

This set is closed because \mathcal{C} is continuous and \le is a closed relation. Since \mathcal{A} is compact, closedness of the graph implies upper hemicontinuity.

Lower hemicontinuity. Let (m_n,\rho_n)\to(m,\rho) and a\in\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{valid}}(m,\rho). By Assumption 4, there exists a^\delta\in\mathcal{A} and \delta>0 such that \mathcal{C}(a^\delta,m)\le\rho-\delta\mathbf{1}. Define a_n=(1-t_n)a+t_n a^\delta with t_n\to0^+ chosen so that \mathcal{C}(a_n,m_n)\le\rho_n. Such t_n exists because \mathcal{C} is continuous and \rho_n\to\rho. Then a_n\to a and a_n\in\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{valid}}(m_n,\rho_n), establishing lower hemicontinuity. \square

A.3 Measurable Selection

Lemma A.3 (Measurable Maximum Theorem). Under Assumptions 2–4, the argmax correspondence

\Gamma^\star(m,\rho)=\arg\max_{a\in\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{valid}}(m,\rho)}\mathbb{E}\left[r(s,a)-\lambda^\top\mathcal{C}(a,m)+\gamma V(m',\rho')\right]

is nonempty, compact-valued, and upper hemicontinuous. Consequently, there exists a measurable selector \pi^\star.

Proof. By Lemma A.2, \Gamma is continuous with compact values. The integrand is continuous in a by Assumption 2 and bounded by Assumption 6. By Berge's Maximum Theorem, \Gamma^\star is nonempty, compact-valued, and upper hemicontinuous. The Measurable Maximum Theorem (Aliprantis–Border, Theorem 18.19) then guarantees the existence of a measurable selector. \square

