Appendix F: Two-Time-Scale Convergence Analysis

F.1 Two-Time-Scale Setup

Let \alpha_t be the fast learning rate and \beta_t the slow learning rate with

\frac{\beta_t}{\alpha_t}\to0,\quad\sum_t\alpha_t=\infty,\quad\sum_t\alpha_t^2<\infty,\quad\sum_t\beta_t=\infty,\quad\sum_t\beta_t^2<\infty.

The fast-scale iterates (\theta_t,\lambda_t) and slow-scale iterates (\phi_t,\theta_{\mathrm{enc},t}) evolve as

\theta_{t+1}=\theta_t+\alpha_t f(\theta_t,\lambda_t,\phi_t,\theta_{\mathrm{enc},t},Z_t),

\lambda_{t+1}=[\lambda_t+\eta_t g(\theta_t,\lambda_t,\phi_t,\theta_{\mathrm{enc},t},Z_t)]_+,

\phi_{t+1}=\phi_t+\beta_t h(\theta_t,\lambda_t,\phi_t,\theta_{\mathrm{enc},t},Z_t),

\theta_{\mathrm{enc},t+1}=\theta_{\mathrm{enc},t}+\beta_t k(\theta_t,\lambda_t,\phi_t,\theta_{\mathrm{enc},t},Z_t),

where Z_t is the Markovian noise.

F.2 Assumptions for Two-Time-Scale SA

Assumption F.1. The functions f,g,h,k are jointly continuous and Lipschitz in their arguments uniformly in Z_t.

Assumption F.2. The Markov chain \{Z_t\} is ergodic with stationary distribution \mu_{\phi,\theta_{\mathrm{enc}}} for fixed (\phi,\theta_{\mathrm{enc}}).

Assumption F.3. The fast-scale ODE

\dot{\theta}=f(\theta,\lambda,\phi,\theta_{\mathrm{enc}}),\qquad
\dot{\lambda}=[g(\theta,\lambda,\phi,\theta_{\mathrm{enc}})]_+

has a unique globally asymptotically stable equilibrium (\theta^\star(\phi,\theta_{\mathrm{enc}}),\lambda^\star(\phi,\theta_{\mathrm{enc}})) for each fixed (\phi,\theta_{\mathrm{enc}}).

F.3 Fast-Scale Convergence

Theorem F.1 (Fast-Scale Convergence). Under Assumptions F.1–F.3 and the Robbins–Monro conditions, for fixed (\phi,\theta_{\mathrm{enc}}),

(\theta_t,\lambda_t)\to(\theta^\star(\phi,\theta_{\mathrm{enc}}),\lambda^\star(\phi,\theta_{\mathrm{enc}}))\quad\text{a.s.}

Proof. This is the standard two-time-scale SA theorem (Borkar, 1997, Chapter 6; Kushner–Yin, 2003). The fast iterates track the ODE with the slow iterates held fixed. \square

F.4 Slow-Scale Convergence

Theorem F.2 (Slow-Scale Convergence). Under Assumptions F.1–F.3, the slow iterates converge to a stationary point of the averaged ODE:

\dot{\phi}=\bar{h}(\phi,\theta_{\mathrm{enc}}),\qquad
\dot{\theta}_{\mathrm{enc}}=\bar{k}(\phi,\theta_{\mathrm{enc}}),

where \bar{h},\bar{k} are the averages of h,k over the stationary distribution \mu_{\phi,\theta_{\mathrm{enc}}}.

Proof. By Theorem F.1, the fast iterates converge to their equilibrium. Substituting into the slow updates and averaging over the stationary distribution yields the result. \square

F.5 Joint Convergence

Theorem F.3 (Joint Convergence). Under Assumptions F.1–F.3 and the two-time-scale separation,

(\theta_t,\lambda_t,\phi_t,\theta_{\mathrm{enc},t})\to(\theta^\star,\lambda^\star,\phi^\star,\theta_{\mathrm{enc}}^\star)\quad\text{a.s.}

Proof. Combine Theorems F.1 and F.2. The fast iterates converge to the equilibrium manifold parameterised by the slow iterates, and the slow iterates converge to a stationary point of the averaged ODE. \square

