Appendix J: Counterexample to the Original Action Selection

Proposition J.1. If \widetilde{\mu}_a is defined as the value

\widetilde{\mu}_a=\mathbb{E}\left[r(s,a)-\lambda^\top\mathcal{C}(a,m)+\gamma V_{\widetilde{\theta}}(m',\rho')\right],

then the rule a_t=\arg\min_{a\in\mathcal{C}_t}\widetilde{\mu}_a is suboptimal.

Proof. Let \mathcal{A}=\{a_1,a_2\} with \widetilde{\mu}_{a_1}=1 and \widetilde{\mu}_{a_2}=2. The optimal action is a_2, but \arg\min selects a_1. \square

Remark J.1. The correct rule is a_t=\arg\max_{a\in\mathcal{C}_t}\widetilde{\mu}_a. If \widetilde{\mu}_a is intended as a cost-to-go, the objective and value function must be redefined accordingly.

